before:
File.ReadAllText returns the file contents as a string. A successful write followed by a read can verify the value you stored; printing a path alone cannot.

This separate temporary-file example appends B after A. AppendAllText(path, text) returns no value; a read-back observes the stored result. The finally block attempts cleanup even if I/O fails:
```csharp
using System;
using System.IO;
string samplePath = Path.GetTempFileName();
try {
    File.WriteAllText(samplePath, "A");
    File.AppendAllText(samplePath, "B");
    Console.WriteLine(File.ReadAllText(samplePath));
}
finally { File.Delete(samplePath); }
```
Output: AB.

Complete local C# example; the app does not execute it:
```csharp
using System;
using System.IO;
string path = Path.GetTempFileName();
try
{
    File.WriteAllText(path, "first");
    File.WriteAllText(path, "second");
    Console.WriteLine(File.ReadAllText(path));
}
catch (IOException) { Console.WriteLine("File operation failed"); }
catch (UnauthorizedAccessException) { Console.WriteLine("Access denied"); }
finally { File.Delete(path); }
```

